troops:
And soft recesses of the sylvan shades.
Now Israel's monarch, and his troops arise,
With peals of shouts ascending to the skies;

To meet the hero in the field of fame.
Ah! fatal meeting to thy troops and thee,
But thou wast deaf to the divine decree;

troubled:
And live on stoutly, as the creatures will
Whose spirits are not troubled by long loves.
A dog would never know me, I’m so changed;

trod:
I saw that one who lost her love in pain,
Who trod on thorns, who drank the loathsome cup;
The lost in night, in day was found again;

The age to brighten every path
By sin and sorrow trod;
For loving hearts to usher in

troubling:
And worst alike shall find and share:
The wicked cease from troubling there,
And there the weary are at rest;

tropic:
Or Arctic creature, dimly stirred
By tropic hint, -- some travelled bird
Imported to the wood;

troubles:
Night is for sorrow and dawn is for joy,
Chasing the troubles that fret and annoy;
Darkness for sighing and daylight for song,--

The gulf of silence, empty even of nought;
Thought’s high-walled maze, which the outed owner troubles
Because the string’s lost and the plan forgot:

troth:
To slacken all those bonds which seemed too soon
And quickly tied to make a lasting troth.
Quick-loving hearts, I thought, may quickly loathe;

trompin:
Less I 'll sen' you off to sleep.
Dis hyeah bah 'd go trompin' 'roun'
Eatin' evahthing he foun';

trottin:
Stumble on, an' mebbe fall;
But I'll keep a-trottin', while you lead on,
Pickin' an' a-trottin', dat's all.

troopin:
Dough it's mighty tryin' fuh to go erway;
But we goes a-troopin' down de ol' wood-track
'Twell dat steamin' kitchen brings us stealin' back,

tromped:
Dese little boots;
Den he tromped de livelong day,
Laffin' in his happy way,

trojan:
Caused our father's primal fall;
And the Trojan War, remember--
'Twas an apple caused it all.

troubadour:
New fingers stir the sod;
A troubadour upon the elm
Betrays the solitude.

trooped:
With her shepherd May,
Flocks and herds trooped at their heels
Along the hill-side way;
